<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Munich</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Munich is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isar">Isar</a>. Founded around 1158 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, it became the residence of the Wittelsbach dukes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> and kings of Bavaria from the 13th century until 1918. In the 20th century it was the birthplace of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> and gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> of 1938, and it is now Germany's third-largest city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/munich.jpg" alt="Munich" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stadtbild_M%C3%BCnchen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At <b>Munich</b> the great powers decided its fate without it, and the army surrendered the fortified Sudetenland without a fight.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the later Middle Ages towns such as <b>Munich</b>, founded by Henry the Lion in 1158, Landshut and Ingolstadt grew under ducal protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the twelfth century Henry the Lion’s foundations shaped the urban map of Germany: Lübeck became the leading city of the Hanseatic League, and <b>Munich</b>, founded in his Bavarian duchy around 1158, became the Bavarian capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1777, when the Bavarian Wittelsbachs died out, Elector Charles Theodore inherited Bavaria, moved his court to <b>Munich</b> and merged the two electorates; Austria's attempt to take part of Bavaria led to the brief War of the Bavarian Succession (1778–1779).</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Brewing was a major industry, and <b>Munich</b>'s breweries pioneered refrigerated lager production.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Albert Einstein was born in Ulm in 1879, though his family soon moved to <b>Munich</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
